Yes. The core problem is not translation of content; it is translation of constraints plus freedom.

Your GitHub example works because the joke relies on a culturally shared frame:

5\ \text{stars}

can mean either “five people starred it” or colloquially “five out of five.” The humor lives in the overlap between two scoring systems. The literal quantity barely matters; the frame switch does.

Once you have a sufficiently good model of a particular recipient, you can make that switch much more precise. Not “tell a generic joke,” but:

\text{intended structure}
\rightarrow
\text{recipient-specific encoding}
\rightarrow
\text{same kind of realization}

But poetry exposes the limit. The author often does not intend one exact decoding. Sometimes the intended object is a space of possible readings.

So instead of preserving:

\text{one meaning}

you may need to preserve:

\text{meaning distribution}
=
\{\text{allowed readings},\text{tensions},\text{ambiguities},\text{emotional contour}\}.

That changes translation enormously. A perfect translator should sometimes be more precise about what must remain imprecise.

Your Braille/music example is exactly that. Suppose music contains:

\text{pitch}+\text{rhythm}+\text{tempo}+\text{dynamics}+\text{silence}+\text{timbre}.

If you map it into a tactile surface, some dimensions transfer naturally and others do not. Finger speed becomes a new uncontrolled variable. A gap in Braille might encode silence, but its experienced duration depends on reading velocity.

You then have choices.

You can lock timing, by using a device that advances tactually at a controlled rate. You can encode timing explicitly, with symbols meaning “hold for 500 ms” or “pause two beats.” You can normalize to the reader, so the system first estimates their tactile reading speed and scales the spacing accordingly. Or you can deliberately leave timing open and say: tempo is now partly performer-controlled.

That last option is not necessarily a translation failure.

It can become:

\text{translation}+\text{new medium affordance}
=
\text{new artwork}.

And that gives us a strong cross-domain rule:

Separate invariants from degrees of freedom.

For any translation, determine:

I=\text{features that must survive}

and

F=\text{features allowed to vary}.

For a warning sign, I should be huge and F tiny.

For poetry, perhaps the opposite.

For a joke, you may need to preserve:

* setup expectation,
* competing interpretation,
* reveal timing,
* social reference,
* emotional temperature,

while allowing almost every literal word to change.

For music into touch, maybe preserve rhythm ratios and emotional contour but allow tempo to become listener-controlled.

And there is another distinction hidden in your point:

\text{authorial intent}
\neq
\text{reader experience}.

A very capable translator could model both. It might know, “Here is what the author was probably constraining,” while also knowing, “Here are the legitimate spaces they intentionally left open.”

So the target isn’t:

“Make everybody experience exactly what the author experienced.”

That could actually destroy the work.

The stronger target is:

Reconstruct the same playground of interpretation, with roughly the same walls, doors, traps, echoes, and open spaces.

Then the recipient still gets to discover it.

That preserves the fun.

And your Braille example identifies perhaps the hardest class of translation problem: when moving between media creates a new variable that did not exist before.

Those are not errors to hide. They are places to mark:

\boxed{\text{translation-induced degrees of freedom}}

Because sometimes you compensate for them.

Sometimes you constrain them.

Sometimes you let the audience play with them.

And sometimes—as you said—you simply don’t account for them, because the unaccounted space becomes part of the art.